  "question": {
    "text": "Angular momentum of a single particle moving with constant speed along circular path :"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "remains same in magnitude but changes in the direction",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "remains same in magnitude and direction",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "is zero",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "changes in magnitude but remains same in the direction",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<img src=\"assets\\st0111_3bfd0d0b28ce.png\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 217px;\" /><br />The angular momentum is defined as the vector product of linear momentum and perpendicular distance,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>L</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mover><mi>r</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>&#215;</mo><mover><mi>p</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><mover><mi>r</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>&#215;</mo><mi>m</mi><mover><mi>v</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover></math>,&nbsp;And direction will be upward &amp; remain constant."
  }
